# C-015 Chart reference confidence — author Freeze2

The Chart caller now preserves its admitted reference calculation while leaving the Moon’s possible signs across an unknown birth time unverified. It sets the existing `moonSignCandidates` field to an empty array before signature/share preparation and uses the existing unresolved-confidence path. The singular Registry Sun bridge is retained only for known-time calculations. No false Sun claim was observed; this removal is a conservative response to missing date-wide coverage proof.

**Frozen source:** base `f803d2543ad81343b22d49326b8b46d0e2ea03a0`, 16 files. `freeze2-identity.json` SHA256 `54cd5acbc7f69c7b3935943a9380be10bb18e8ca2b5a90c1d1766625d8bca55c`; `freeze2.patch` SHA256 `b13527901ee962d462b0ddd2bdbc16f82763ffa8ad7486d797358b46f76fd0a4`.

Eight product files change: ChartCalculator; one added key in each of six existing catalogs; comments in chart-date-certainty. Eight test/driver files cover the actual caller, consumers, Registry/ownership behavior, key counts, sharing and the former endpoint-call expectation. Removing the added locale line restores every original catalog byte. A TypeScript AST comparison proves chart-date-certainty executable code unchanged. No SDK, endpoint algorithm, schema, account format, MoonPhaseTool, global styles, navigation, Registry/Astrofolio source or root documentation changes are included.

## Observed behavior and exact preservation

The native driver uses the actual Preact caller, actual rc.6 calculation/receipt modules, controlled synthetic city selection and loader/call observation. Its optional baseline overlays only the exact delivered f803 ChartCalculator. Shared numerical/receipt code is unchanged. The controlled coordinates are synthetic; the historical timezone/date membership and geocentric body values are real engine/Intl outputs.

Six same-Chrome unknown-time comparisons preserve the exact native Chart JSON, compact numerical chart JSON after removing only the intentionally changed candidate metadata, immutable envelope JSON, actual downloaded receipt bytes and positions token. Toronto, Juneau, an ordinary singleton date, an ordinary two-sign date, a non-hour gap date and repeated Apia are covered. Known-time full/Moon/Rising paths also retain their exact own-path outputs and Registry behavior. Full mode executes its one public natal path; Moon/Rising retain their existing one legacy natal path. The former Chart endpoint-confidence calls are absent; this does not mean the primary calculation performs no ephemeris work.

Concrete counterexamples remain visible in `native-summary.json` and the complete reports:

- Toronto 1919-03-31: the previous `[aries]` claim omitted the member instant 1919-03-31T04:30Z, whose Moon is Pisces (Chrome longitude 359.7145003582242°). The result now carries `[]`.
- Juneau 1867-10-18: the previous `[gemini]` claim omitted the returned-date member 1867-10-19T00:31:13Z, whose Moon is Cancer (97.12696569323725°). The result now carries `[]`.
- Ordinary Bangkok and London controls deliberately also withhold a verified range: endpoint agreement or disagreement alone is no longer promoted to date-wide evidence. No wider sampling/completeness assertion is introduced.

Actual consumer checks confirm unresolved Moon context, no definitive Moon approach/communication advice, no Moon communication aspects, existing signature filtering, scene propagation and unverified sharing labels. Positions wire bytes remain unchanged; the receiver’s existing `angles:null` rule keeps Moon confidence unresolved without adding a schema field. Actual normal-build sharing checks retain the Moon image/dialog uncertainty label, existing possibility warning and reference-position export behavior. Existing reference Sun values in post-chart events/context and save-related context are explicitly unchanged; disabling the Registry bridge is not whole-product Sun verification.

The new six-locale notice says possible Moon signs across the date have not been verified, replacing Chart’s previous assertion that the Moon changed signs and that both neighbors covered the answer. Existing layout/classes are retained. C014 refused dates still fail before numerical/receipt/endpoint work; recovery and typed fields/focus work. Known-time behavior, positions-only import and current ownership fences remain intact.

## Executed author checks

- Node 22.23.2 and Node 24.19.0: 93 focused controls pass on each. Node22 has no native Temporal.
- Actual Chrome 152.0.7977.83 confidence fixture: 20/20 groups pass, including six exact baseline parities, six localized keyboard downloads, three known-time modes, C014 refusal/recovery, three loader ownership cases and positions-only import. 861 owned-origin GET requests; no non-GET or external request. Contexts/browser/server closed.
- Existing C014 native caller gate: 35/35 pass. Existing actual normal-build Chart ownership gate: 17/17 pass.
- Existing `tests/t17-positions-share.mjs` normal-build gate passes, including images, dialog, native sharing, unknown-Moon presentation and receiver flows. Its summary and raw output are retained.
- Normal build passes all postbuild gates; full engine static closure remains 23.3 KB under the unchanged 25 KB budget. Astro check: 0 errors, 0 warnings, 11 existing hints. Strict project TypeScript passes.
- Final complete candidate suite: **5,039 tests pass, one test fails**. The remaining failure correctly rejects the now-stale Phase1 capture source fingerprint after adding six catalog keys. The exact baseline passes that evidence test. Root owns the fresh captures/evidence carrier; this author report does not call the branch release-ready.
- Two initially blocked numerical test suites were reproduced on the exact baseline with the same linked module failure. An isolated local copy of the already-installed Astronomy Engine package, with all eight file hashes identical, resolves the scratch path problem; all 67 formerly blocked controls and the final full suite execute. No install or root dependency mutation occurred.

## Preserved failures and boundaries

All initial execution failures are retained. Four old assertions failed after the policy change: Registry endpoint source coupling, simulated endpoint-failure ownership, and two catalog counts. The replacement ownership regression uses the still-real local-date-reference refusal boundary. Initial full suite failures for missing scratch dist disappear after the normal build. The author’s early AST extraction and type mistakes are retained in logs. Four native harness attempts are retained with their exact saved driver versions: container instead of button; hidden More action and non-full receipt assumptions; incorrect full-path count in Moon mode; and nonexistent unknown-time checkbox in Rising. Final fixtures use the actual existing controls and paths; no product fix, numerical tolerance or gate timeout change was needed for these mistakes.

Provisional Freeze1 was rejected before independent delivery because two new tests compared the legacy adapter’s original input property order with the portable adapter’s normalized snapshot order. Freeze2 changes only that comparator to the actual existing legacy path; both preserve exact bytes within their own path. The native f803-versus-candidate same-path controls pass without normalization. Some early native reports read `driverSha256` at process completion while later harness edits were underway: use the retained `native-first/second/third/fourth-driver.mjs` snapshot mappings in COMMANDS, not those late-read fields, for historical driver identity. The final accepted driver was unchanged during execution and is in Freeze2. All 16 frozen files are rehashed unchanged after all checks.

This is author implementation/acceptance evidence, not independent review, production deployment, SDK publication or adoption. The finite member observations prove counterexamples, not full-date coverage or an independent ephemeris oracle. C014 point membership remains only a reference-time admission check. The existing local-noon caption can overstate the clock after a gap shift; Chart Moon-mode phase-at-birth wording, MoonPhaseTool alternate-sign inference and reference Sun personalization remain separately open. No claim is made that those wider product boundaries were corrected here.
